Used in the graph:

  - OpenStreetMap (https://www.openstreetmap.org/copyright)
    Map data © OpenStreetMap contributors, licensed ODbL-1.0.
    Geofabrik extract new-york-261001.osm.pbf (data timestamp
    2026-10-01T20:22:06Z). Used for footways, crossings, steps, streets
    and topology.

  - NYC DOT Pedestrian Ramp Locations (dataset id: ufzp-rrqu)
    https://data.cityofnewyork.us/Transportation/Pedestrian-Ramp-Locations/ufzp-rrqu
    Collected for NYC DOT by a contractor (Cyclomedia) from vehicle-mounted
    street-level imagery and LiDAR; records captured March 2017 to January
    2020, mostly in 2018. DOT says the measurements do not establish ADA
    compliance. NYC Open Data terms of use, no licence attached. Used for
    curb-ramp locations, slopes and warning-surface status.

  - NYC Planimetric Database: Sidewalks (dataset id: 52n9-sdep), NYC OTI
    https://data.cityofnewyork.us/City-Government/NYC-Planimetric-Database-Sidewalk/52n9-sdep
    NYC Open Data terms of use, no licence attached. Used for sidewalk
    widths and as the source of the gap-fill centrelines shipped beside
    the graph.

  - Borough boundaries: OpenStreetMap, fetched through Nominatim. The
    pipeline first tries NYC Borough Boundaries (dataset id: 7t3b-ywvw),
    which NYC Open Data has withdrawn, so the fallback is what ships.

  - NYC 2017 Topobathymetric LiDAR bare-earth DTM (NYC OTI)
    Served by the NY State GIS Program Office ArcGIS ImageServer
    (https://elevation.its.ny.gov/, NYC_TopoBathymetric_2017_1_meter).
    Public data, no licence attached. Fetched as 2 m tiles to estimate
    node elevations and edge inclines.

  - LiDAR point clouds: the 2017 NYC topobathymetric survey (NYC OTI) and
    the 2014 USGS survey of New York City, served as Entwine Point Tiles on
    NOAA's Digital Coast archive
    (https://noaa-nos-coastal-lidar-pds.s3.amazonaws.com/entwine/geoid18/9306/ept.json
    and .../4920/ept.json). Published with no licence attached. Used for
    deck heights on bridges and elevated ways.

